/year><article-title>Poor correlation between BCG vaccination-induced T cell responses and protection against tuberculosis</article-title><source>Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A</source><volume>104</volume><fpage>12434</fpage><lpage>12439</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17640915</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn id="n2"><p>Willem A. Hanekom is with the South African Tuberculosis Vaccine Initiative, Institute of Infectious Diseases and Molecular Medicine, University of Cape Town, Cape Town, Western Cape, South Africa. Hazel M. Dockrell is with the Department of Infectious and Tropical Diseases, London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine, London, United Kingdom. Tom H. M. Ottenhoff is with the Department of Immunohematology &#x00026; Blood Transfusion, and Department of Infectious Diseases, Leiden University Medical Center, Leiden, The Netherlands. T. Mark Doherty is with the Department of Infectious Disease Immunology, Statens Serum Institute, Copenhagen, Denmark. Helen Fletcher and Helen McShane are with the Centre for Clinical Vaccinology and Tropical Medicine, University of Oxford, Oxford, United Kingdom. Frank F. Weichold is with the Aeras Global Tuberculosis Vaccine Foundation, Bethesda, Maryland, United States of America. Dan F. Hoft is with the Division of Immunobiology, Departments of Internal Medicine and Molecular Microbiology, Saint Louis University Health Sciences Center, St. Louis, Missouri, United States of America. Shreemanta K. Parida is with the Department of Immunology, Max Planck Institute for Infection Biology, Berlin, Germany. Uli J. Fruth is with the Initiative for Vaccine Research, World Health Organization, Geneva, Switzerland.</p></fn><fn id="ack1" fn-type="con"><p><bold>Author contributions.</bold> All authors attended at least one of the three WHO committee meetings, and all participated in preparation of this manuscript.</p></fn><fn id="fn1" fn-type="financial-disclosure"><p><bold>Funding:</bold> The World Health Organization Initiative for Vaccine Research sponsored the three meetings that formed the basis of this manuscript. The authors alone are responsible for the views expressed in this publication, which do not necessarily represent the decisions, policies, or views of the World Health Organization. The following authors are supported by the Bill &#x00026; Melinda Gates Foundation: WAH, HMD, THMO, TMD, FFW, and SKP through Grand Challenges in Global Health Grant &#x00023;37772 (&#x0201c;Biomarkers of Protective Immunity against TB in the context of HIV/AIDS in Africa&#x0201d;), and WAH, THMO, TMD, and FFW through Grant &#x00023;37885 (&#x0201c;Preclinical and Clinical Evaluation of a Post-Exposure TB Vaccine&#x0201d;). WAH, HMD, THMO, TMD, and HM are also supported by the European Commission, within the 6th Framework Programme. (The text represents the authors' views and does not necessarily represent a position of the Commission, who will not be liable for the use made of such information.) WAH and DFH are supported by the National Institutes of Health (WAH: RO1-AI065653 and NO1-AI70022; DFH: NO1-AI25464, NO1-AI70022, and RO1-AI48391). WAH is also supported by the Aeras Global Tuberculosis Vaccine Foundation and the European and Developing Countries Trials Partnership. HM is a Wellcome Trust Senior Clinical Research Fellow. No specific funding was received for this manuscript.</p></fn><fn id="n3" fn-type="conflict"><p><bold>Competing Interests:</bold> The authors have declared that no competing interests exist.</p></fn></fn-group></back></article>